  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 320 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nStronger southwest flow and sunny skies to start the day will allow \nhighs Thursday to reach the lower 80s, our warmest day of the week. \nThe front sags south out of northern Missouri Thursday, and precip \nchances will return to central Missouri. This could impact the \nnorthern fringes of the area, but is unlikely.\n\nFriday looks to be the beginning of the end for this unseasonable \nsynoptic ridge. A deeper low approaches, finally beginning the \nbreakdown of high pressure, and bringing widespread rain chances \nthat begin to move into the western portions of the area by Friday \nevening. Peak PoPs currently line up throughout Saturday morning and \nearly afternoon, 50-60% area-wide. Some models begin to taper the \nintensity and coverage by trick-or-treating hours while others show \nwidespread rain continuing into the evening. Though there remains \nuncertainty on the details at this lead time, it is safe to say that \nit may still be raining in some areas and will be gloomy/damp for \nmost.\n\nThe silver lining is that this pattern change is not being brought \nabout by a front, and the temperature of the airmass is not expected \nto drastically change. Cloud cover and rainfall get us down closer \nto 70 for the daytime high, but lows remain in the mid-50s. More \ntroughing and shortwaves are progged to follow throughout the \nweekend, with long range models showing the potential for a proper \nfrontal passage complete with seasonal temperatures in the first \nweek of November. Fingers crossed!",
